      "steps": [
        {
          "step_number": 1,
          "title": "Step 1",
          "instruction": "Rinse the baingan (eggplant or aubergine) in water. Pat dry with a kitchen napkin. Apply some oil all over and"
        },
        {
          "step_number": 2,
          "title": "Step 2",
          "instruction": "keep it for roasting on an open flame. You can also grill the baingan or roast in the oven. But then you won't get"
        },
        {
          "step_number": 3,
          "title": "Step 3",
          "instruction": "the smoky flavor of the baingan. Keep the eggplant turning after a 2 to 3 minutes on the flame, so that its evenly"
        },
        {
          "step_number": 4,
          "title": "Step 4",
          "instruction": "cooked. You could also embed some garlic cloves in the baingan and then roast it."
        },
        {
          "step_number": 5,
          "title": "Step 5",
          "instruction": "2. Roast the aubergine till its completely cooked and tender. With a knife check the doneness. The knife should slid"
        },
        {
          "step_number": 6,
          "title": "Step 6",
          "instruction": "easily in aubergines without any resistance. Remove the baingan and immerse in a bowl of water till it cools"
        },
        {
          "step_number": 7,
          "title": "Step 7",
          "instruction": "down."
        },
        {
          "step_number": 8,
          "title": "Step 8",
          "instruction": "3. You can also do the dhungar technique of infusing charcoal smoky flavor in the baingan. This is an optional step."
        },
        {
          "step_number": 9,
          "title": "Step 9",
          "instruction": "Use natural charcoal for this method. Heat a small piece of charcoal on flame till it becomes smoking hot and red."
        },
        {
          "step_number": 10,
          "title": "Step 10",
          "instruction": "4. Make small cuts on the baingan with a knife. Place the red hot charcoal in the same plate where the roasted"
        },
        {
          "step_number": 11,
          "title": "Step 11",
          "instruction": "aubergine is kept. Add a few drops of oil on the charcoal. The charcoal would begin to smoke."
        },
        {
          "step_number": 12,
          "title": "Step 12",
          "instruction": "5. As soon as smoke begins to release from the charcoal, cover the entire plate tightly with a large bowl. Allow the"
        },
        {
          "step_number": 13,
          "title": "Step 13",
          "instruction": "charcoal smoke to get infused for 1 to 2 minutes. The more you do, the more smoky the baingan bharta will"
        },
        {
          "step_number": 14,
          "title": "Step 14",
          "instruction": "become. I just keep for a minute. Alternatively, you can also do this dhungar method once the baingan bharta is"
        }
      ]
    },
